    </div>

    <!-- Engineering Track -->
    <div class="f1g-track" id="f1g-track-eng">
      <div class="f1g-timeline">

        <div class="f1g-phase f1g-phase--now">
          <div class="f1g-phase-dot"></div>
          <div class="f1g-phase-content">
            <div class="f1g-phase-label">NOW — 11th Grade (2026-2027)</div>
            <h4 class="f1g-phase-title">Build the foundation</h4>
            <ul class="f1g-phase-list">
              <li>Take <strong>AP Physics C: Mechanics</strong> and <strong>AP Physics C: Electricity &amp; Magnetism</strong> — these are the direct prerequisites for university fluid dynamics and electromagnetism</li>
              <li>Take <strong>AP Calculus BC</strong> — university aero and dynamics courses assume multivariable calculus from day one</li>
              <li>Robotics (FIRST, which you already do) is directly relevant: controls, mechanical design, systems thinking — name it prominently on university applications</li>
              <li>Research <strong>Formula SAE</strong> teams at your target universities — know which schools have active teams before applying</li>
              <li>Watch: MIT OpenCourseWare 16.100 Aerodynamics — free intro to the actual subject area</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="f1g-phase">
          <div class="f1g-phase-dot"></div>
          <div class="f1g-phase-content">
            <div class="f1g-phase-label">UNDERGRAD — 2027 to 2031 (~4 years)</div>
            <h4 class="f1g-phase-title">Aerospace or Mechanical Engineering degree</h4>
            <p class="f1g-phase-sub">Target universities: Georgia Tech, Michigan, Purdue, MIT, or Cornell. For NJ cost-effectiveness: Rutgers or Princeton.</p>
            <ul class="f1g-phase-list">
              <li><strong>Join Formula SAE in semester 1</strong> — this is the most important extracurricular action you can take. Work in aero, vehicle dynamics, or electronics sub-teams. F1 recruiters treat this as near-equivalent to an internship.</li>
              <li>Focus coursework on: fluid dynamics, heat transfer, structural mechanics, controls systems, MATLAB / Python</li>
              <li>Pursue an aerospace or automotive internship in years 2-3 (aerospace OEMs, NASA, DoD labs all build directly applicable skills)</li>
              <li>Aim for strong GPA in core engineering modules — especially fluid mechanics and computational methods</li>
              <li>If at a UK university (Southampton, Loughborough): the 4-year MEng with industrial placement year gives an integrated Masters + trackside experience in one degree</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="f1g-phase">
          <div class="f1g-phase-dot"></div>
          <div class="f1g-phase-content">
            <div class="f1g-phase-label">MASTERS — 2031 to 2032 (1-2 years)</div>
            <h4 class="f1g-phase-title">Motorsport or aerospace Masters in the UK</h4>
            <p class="f1g-phase-sub">This is the step that moves you from "strong engineer" to "F1 hire." Most UK Masters include industry projects placed directly inside F1 teams.</p>
            <ul class="f1g-phase-list">
              <li><strong>Aero / CFD path</strong>: Southampton MSc Race Car Aerodynamics — the most direct route to an F1 aero role in the world</li>
              <li><strong>Broad engineering path</strong>: Cranfield MSc Advanced Motorsport Engineering — covers aero + VD + powertrain + electronics in one year; best if you want flexibility across departments</li>
              <li><strong>Vehicle dynamics path</strong>: Oxford Brookes MSc Motorsport Engineering — physically inside Motorsport Valley, Red Bull placement partner</li>
              <li><strong>Budget-conscious path</strong>: TU Delft MSc Aerospace (Netherlands) — comparable F1 hiring rates, lower cost than UK, strong CFD research</li>
              <li>Apply to F1 early careers / graduate positions in <strong>August-October</strong> — this is the critical window for the following year's intake</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="f1g-phase f1g-phase--f1">
          <div class="f1g-phase-dot"></div>
          <div class="f1g-phase-content">
            <div class="f1g-phase-label">F1 ENTRY — 2032-2033 (Age ~23-24)</div>
            <h4 class="f1g-phase-title">First role on the grid</h4>
            <ul class="f1g-phase-list">
              <li>Entry title: Junior Aerodynamicist / Junior CFD Analyst / Junior Vehicle Dynamics Engineer / Junior Performance Engineer</li>
              <li><strong>UK work visa</strong>: After graduating from a top-50 globally ranked US university (MIT, Georgia Tech, Michigan all qualify), you are eligible for the <strong>UK High Potential Individual (HPI) Visa</strong> — 2-3 years of unsponsored work authorization. This is a major advantage: you do not need the F1 team to sponsor you from day one.</li>
              <li>Starting salaries: £30,000-£45,000 for UK-based junior roles (2026 market)</li>
              <li>US-based entry: Consider Haas F1 (Kannapolis, NC) or Cadillac F1 (Indianapolis + Silverstone) for roles that do not require immediate relocation to the UK</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Tech Track -->
    <div class="f1g-track f1g-track--hide" id="f1g-track-tech">
